new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3df52f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+## Deployment
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) configuration for running everything on a single Linux server: one Docker container with the Rails app, the SQLite databases, and the background jobs, with SSL termination handled by kamal-proxy.
+
+### What you need
+
+- A Linux server with Docker installed, reachable over SSH (Kamal logs in as `root` by default).
+- A hostname whose DNS points at your server. kamal-proxy uses it to get a free SSL certificate from Let's Encrypt.
+- A Docker registry account to hold the Fizzy image. [Docker Hub](https://hub.docker.com) works out of the box.
+- An SMTP server for outgoing mail (magic links and notifications). Any email provider that offers SMTP credentials works.
+- Ruby and Bundler on your own machine, so you can run `bin/kamal` from your checkout (`bundle install` first, or `bin/setup`).
+
+### Configuring
+
+Edit the two deployment files:
+
+1. `config/deploy.yml` — fill in the TODOs:
+   - `servers.web.hosts`: your server's IP address.
+   - `proxy.host`: your hostname, e.g. `fizzy.example.com`.
+   - `registry.username`: your Docker registry username.
+   - `env.clear`: your SMTP server settings. Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` too if your provider requires a specific sender.
+2. `.kamal/secrets` — fill in each value:
+   - `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD`: your Docker registry password or access token.
+   - `SECRET_KEY_BASE`: generate with `bin/rails secret`.
+   - `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`: generate a pair with:
+
+     ```sh
+     bin/rails runner "key = WebPush.generate_key; puts \"VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}\nVAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}\""
+     ```
+   - `SMTP_PASSWORD`: the password for your SMTP server.
+
+   Instead of pasting values in, you can reference environment variables (`SECRET_KEY_BASE=$SECRET_KEY_BASE`) or call out to a password manager (`SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(op read op://Vault/Fizzy/key)`). Either way, don't commit real secrets to git.
+
+### Deploying
+
+From your checkout, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This builds the Docker image, pushes it to your registry, boots kamal-proxy with SSL for your hostname, prepares the SQLite databases, and starts the app. When it's done, Fizzy is live at `https://<your hostname>` — sign up and you're in.
+
+Later updates are just:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+### Where the data lives
+
+The SQLite databases and uploaded files are stored in `/rails/storage` inside the container, which is backed by a Docker volume named `fizzy`. The volume survives deploys, so your data sticks around. To back up Fizzy, back up that volume (see `docker volume inspect fizzy` on the server for its location).
+
+### Background jobs
+
+Jobs run inside the same container as the web server, via Solid Queue in Puma (see `config/puma.rb`). No extra services are needed. If you ever want to turn that off, set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=false` and run a dedicated job role with `cmd: bin/jobs` instead.
+
+### Day to day
+
+- `bin/kamal console` — a Rails console on the server.
+- `bin/kamal shell` — a bash shell in the running container.
+- `bin/kamal app logs -f` — follow the app logs.
+- `bin/kamal rollback <version>` — roll back to a previous version.
+
+If you don't log in to your server as `root`, set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`. That user needs Docker access.
